{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS3Niy89SN9bbUt2gNfG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed all three causes in the code and wrote them up in `ANSWER.md`. I only syntax-checked `server.py`. I didn't run the server or the browser flow, and I couldn't reach the real Intervals.icu, so the end-to-end connect is untested.\n\n**Causes**\n1. **Connect failed for everyone.** The button was a plain link to `GET /api/intervals/auth/init`. A full-page navigation doesn't send the `Authorization` header, so every request got a 401. Init is now an authenticated `POST` that returns `{authorize_url}`, and the page calls it with `fetch`, then redirects the browser to that URL.\n2. **The pasted authorize link returned 400.** The calendar sync added `ACTIVITY:READ` and `CALENDAR:READ` next to the `:WRITE` scopes. Intervals appears to reject an area named twice, since `:WRITE` already includes `:READ`. The scopes are now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. The 400 body doesn't name the parameter, so this is the best fit for the symptoms rather than something I confirmed.\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after an hour.** Intervals tokens have no `refresh_token` and no `expires_in`. They last until the athlete revokes them. The code invented a one-hour expiry, and the background refresher then marked the connection expired. I removed that expiry. A 401 from Intervals now marks the connection \"expired, reconnect\".\n\n**Your colleague's note**\n- **Used:** the no-refresh-token finding, the duplicate-scope rule, and the POST init with a browser redirect.\n- **Not used:**\n  - Dropping `grant_type` and `redirect_uri` from the token exchange. Rivera's logged exchange worked with them.\n  - A JWT `state` and an account-mismatch guard. The existing signed `state` already carries the user.\n  - Encrypting the token at rest. The store here is in-memory, and the production storage isn't in this repo.\n\